% ============================================================================
% hurricane-categories — the Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale: each category
% (1..5) → the DAMAGE descriptor the source uses for it (adj-facts-stdlib,
% METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% When a hurricane is coming, the single number everyone hears first is its
% CATEGORY. The Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale sorts a storm into one of
% five categories by its sustained wind speed, and — the part this table
% captures — the National Hurricane Center pairs each category with a short
% DAMAGE descriptor: a category 1 will produce "some damage", a category 3
% brings "devastating damage", a category 4 or 5 brings "catastrophic damage".
% Before you can reason about evacuation, storm surge, or how a "major
% hurricane" (category 3 and up) differs from a lesser one, you must first know
% WHICH damage descriptor the source attaches to a given category. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? damage_level(category_3, $Descriptor)     % devastating_damage
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `damage_level(category, descriptor)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% descriptor WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of the five Saffir-Simpson categories (it never
% invents a descriptor).
%
% Each of the five categories and the descriptor word the source states for it,
% as a little truth table:
%
%     category      descriptor (source's own word)   the NHC summary phrase
%     -----------   ------------------------------   ---------------------------
%     category_1    some_damage                      "will produce some damage"
%     category_2    extensive_damage                 "will cause extensive damage"
%     category_3    devastating_damage               "Devastating damage will occur"
%     category_4    catastrophic_damage              "Catastrophic damage will occur"
%     category_5    catastrophic_damage              "Catastrophic damage will occur"
%
% The relation also runs BACKWARD — bind a descriptor and recall which
% category (or categories) the source gives it:
%
%     ? damage_level($Category, catastrophic_damage)   % category_4 AND category_5
%
% Note the honest DUPLICATE: category 4 and category 5 BOTH carry
% `catastrophic_damage`, because the NHC source opens the category-4 AND the
% category-5 paragraph with the identical sentence "Catastrophic damage will
% occur." We keep both rows exactly as the source states them — we do NOT invent
% a stronger word for category 5 that the source does not use. So the backward
% query above honestly returns TWO categories, which is the truth of the scale.
%
% A key that is NOT one of the five Saffir-Simpson categories — `category_6`
% (there is no category 6), a `tropical_storm`, or a `tropical_depression`
% (weaker systems the Saffir-Simpson scale does not rank) — is deliberately NOT
% a row, so a category recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a damage
% word. That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a
% reasoner on: it answers exactly the category→descriptor pairs it can ground,
% and only those.
%
% The `descriptor` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be
% the damage word the source EXPLICITLY uses for that category — never one the
% source does not support. The atom is the source's summary damage phrase
% collapsed to snake_case: "some damage" → `some_damage`, "extensive damage" →
% `extensive_damage`, "Devastating damage" → `devastating_damage`,
% "Catastrophic damage" → `catastrophic_damage`. That literal fidelity is why
% category 5's cell is `catastrophic_damage` and not some invented superlative.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every category→descriptor
% pair below was read out of the fetched NOAA / National Hurricane Center
% "Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale" page, and any pair whose descriptor word
% could not be verified there would have been dropped). All five spans come from
% the SAME primary source, https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/aboutsshws.php :
%
%   category_1 → some_damage
%     "Very dangerous winds will produce some damage: Well-constructed frame
%      homes could have damage to roof, shingles, vinyl siding and gutters."
%
%   category_2 → extensive_damage
%     "Extremely dangerous winds will cause extensive damage: Well-constructed
%      frame homes could sustain major roof and siding damage."
%
%   category_3 → devastating_damage
%     "Devastating damage will occur: Well-built framed homes may incur major
%      damage or removal of roof decking and gable ends."
%
%   category_4 → catastrophic_damage
%     "Catastrophic damage will occur: Well-built framed homes can sustain
%      severe damage with loss of most of the roof structure and/or some
%      exterior walls."
%
%   category_5 → catastrophic_damage
%     "Catastrophic damage will occur: A high percentage of framed homes will be
%      destroyed, with total roof failure and wall collapse."
%
% The five spans quoted above are the row `source` values themselves, so each
% descriptor is checkable from the row that states it rather than from a list
% in this header.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The
% envelope used to be the category-1 span, so a category-3, -4 or -5
% answer was warranted primarily by a sentence about category 1, and the
% other four spans reached no answer at all. Each row now carries the NHC
% sentence stating its own damage phrase, and the envelope is the page's
% scale-defining sentence, which names no category and no damage value:
%
%     "The Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale estimates potential property
%      damage." -- the page's own scale-defining sentence
%
% THE HONEST DUPLICATE SURVIVES. category_4 and category_5 still share the
% value `catastrophic_damage`, because the page opens both paragraphs with
% "Catastrophic damage will occur." -- but their SPANS differ, so each row
% now cites the paragraph that is actually about it rather than both
% answers sharing one sentence.
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the
% same host returns 404 and holds none of these spans), tags removed
% without a space and only ASCII whitespace collapsed: each row's span
% occurs exactly once, inside one `<td>`, scripts set aside and over the
% whole file; the envelope occurs exactly once, inside one `<p>`, with no
% `<head>` copy. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row restates a
% `locator` line or a `trust` tier.
%
% All spans are from the same NOAA / National Hurricane Center page,
% a primary U.S. government source, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table damage_level {
    columns category, descriptor

    row (category_1, some_damage) {
        source "Very dangerous winds will produce some damage: Well-constructed frame homes could have damage to roof, shingles, vinyl siding and gutters."
    }
    row (category_2, extensive_damage) {
        source "Extremely dangerous winds will cause extensive damage: Well-constructed frame homes could sustain major roof and siding damage."
    }
    row (category_3, devastating_damage) {
        source "Devastating damage will occur: Well-built framed homes may incur major damage or removal of roof decking and gable ends."
    }
    row (category_4, catastrophic_damage) {
        source "Catastrophic damage will occur: Well-built framed homes can sustain severe damage with loss of most of the roof structure and/or some exterior walls."
    }
    row (category_5, catastrophic_damage) {
        source "Catastrophic damage will occur: A high percentage of framed homes will be destroyed, with total roof failure and wall collapse."
    }

    source "The Saffir-Simpson Hurricane Wind Scale estimates potential property damage."
    locator "https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/aboutsshws.php"
    trust authoritative
}
